# Windows CLI Basics – TryHackMe

## 📌 Overview
This repository contains my notes and hands-on exercises from the **Windows CLI Basics** room on TryHackMe. The lab introduces the Windows Command Prompt (CMD) and covers essential command-line operations used for file management, system administration, and basic system enumeration. It provides a strong foundation for anyone beginning Windows administration or cybersecurity.

---

## 🎯 Objectives
- Learn the basics of the Windows Command Prompt (CMD)
- Navigate the Windows file system using CLI
- Manage files and directories
- Gather system and network information
- Understand commonly used Windows command-line utilities

---

## 🛠️ Skills Learned
- Windows Command Prompt (CMD)
- File and directory navigation
- File management
- System enumeration
- Network information gathering
- Basic Windows administration

---

## 📚 Commands Practiced

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `dir` | List files and directories |
| `cd` | Change the current directory |
| `cd..` | Move to the parent directory |
| `tree` | Display the folder structure |
| `mkdir` | Create a new directory |
| `rmdir` | Remove a directory |
| `copy` | Copy files |
| `move` | Move files or folders |
| `del` | Delete files |
| `rename` | Rename files or directories |
| `type` | Display the contents of a text file |
| `more` | View file contents one page at a time |
| `find` | Search for text within files |
| `where` | Locate executable files |
| `hostname` | Display the computer name |
| `whoami` | Show the current logged-in user |
| `systeminfo` | Display detailed system information |
| `ipconfig` | View network configuration |
| `cls` | Clear the Command Prompt screen |
| `help` | Display help information for commands |

---

## 🧪 Key Takeaways
- Navigated the Windows file system entirely through the command line.
- Practiced creating, moving, copying, renaming, and deleting files and folders.
- Retrieved system and network information using built-in Windows commands.
- Learned essential CMD commands commonly used in IT support and cybersecurity.
- Improved confidence working with Windows without relying on the graphical interface.

